window.docContents[203].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations":{"contents":"The formula  in\n combines a choice of coordinate derivatives\nwith assumptions under which they exist. We fix those operators and state the\nregularity needed to interpret them classically.\n\nThe starting point is the Fréchet derivative, the linear first-order\napproximation that carries the chain rule, the inverse function theorem, and\nvariational calculus in normed spaces. Out of that single notion we build the\ngradient, divergence, and Laplacian as finite coordinate operations, so that\n\" is harmonic\" unfolds all the way down to iterated Fréchet\nderivatives along basis directions. The\nverified computer algebra system of the computation chapter uses the same\nconventions. Asking it for the Laplacian of the model harmonic polynomial\n\n-- Check the planar Laplacian using the\n-- coordinate conventions fixed below.\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 2 - y ^ 2)\n\n\nproduces a checked zero residual, rendered from the compiler's own output:\n\n#cas [laplacian x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ 2 - y ^ 2)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe calculation certifies  for the same coordinate\nLaplacian used in the later analytic statements.\n\nOn the unit disk, this polynomial is harmonic but does not solve the\nzero-boundary Dirichlet problem: its boundary values at  and\n are  and . On the circle\n, its boundary datum is\n. The residual calculation verifies the interior equation;\nthe boundary calculation determines which Dirichlet problem it solves.\n\nIn a polynomial heat solution,\nthe spatial Laplacian instead balances a time derivative.\n\nA residual can vanish at every point while the boundary condition is\nstill unproved. A derivative term exists in Lean whether or not the function is\ndifferentiable, because fderiv is a total function. A distributional identity\ncan hold without a locally integrable representative. The solution predicates\nstate separately which identities and regularity conditions are required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations","header":"3.1. Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior","header":"6.7.1. Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-canonical-real-subspace":{"contents":"An  function is real when its imaginary part\nvanishes almost everywhere.  The real Sobolev space is the real submoduleIt is an -submodule, not a -submodule: multiplication\nby  does not preserve real-valuedness.  It is the kernel of the bounded\nimaginary-part map, hence a closed and complete real Hilbert subspace.\nThe subtype HsSpaceReal is the real structure used by the integer bridge\nand the restriction space; realness of the physical representative is\nexpressed by the characterization theorems below.\n\nRealness is defined through the Bessel representative toLp u, whereas the\nweak calculus sees the physical representative toL2Fun u. On the frequency\nside the two differ by multiplication with the symbol , so comparing\nthem calls for a frequency-side description of real-valuedness.\n\nReal-valuedness in physical space is equivalent to Hermitian symmetry in\nfrequency space:\n\nThe  Fourier transform intertwines complex conjugation with conjugation\nand reflection in frequency.  Real-valued functions are exactly the fixed\npoints of complex conjugation, so applying the Fourier equivalence gives the\nstated Hermitian-symmetry characterization.\n\nSince  is real, even, and nowhere zero, multiplication by it\npreserves Hermitian symmetry in both directions. Realness of the Bessel\nrepresentative and realness of the physical representative are therefore the\nsame condition whenever .\n\nWhen , the Bessel representative is real if and only if the physical\n representative is real.  Consequently,The physical representative of an element in the real subspace therefore\nbelongs to real ; this realization is a bounded real-linear map.\n\nLowering from order  to order zero multiplies the Fourier representative\nby the positive, even, nonvanishing symbol .  Such a multiplier\npreserves and reflects Hermitian symmetry.  Apply the Fourier characterization\nof real-valuedness on both sides.\n\nRead from right to left, the equivalence is a constructor: an element known to\nhave a real physical representative belongs to the real subspace, with no\nseparate argument about its Bessel representative.\n\nFor , an element  together with a\nproof that its physical representative is real can be packaged directly asThe reverse maps and the concrete whole-space extensions are built with this\nconstructor.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.3. The canonical real subspace","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-canonical-real-subspace"}});